1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,655
Nuremberg, Germany.

2
00:00:01,875 --> 00:00:04,254
May twenty sixth, eighteen twenty eight.

3
00:00:04,474 --> 00:00:10,283
A teenage boy stumbles into the town square, walking like he's never walked on a street before.

4
00:00:10,783 --> 00:00:11,960
He's holding a letter.

5
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:13,435
He can barely speak.

6
00:00:13,655 --> 00:00:18,001
When people ask him questions, he repeats one sentence, over and over.

7
00:00:18,501 --> 00:00:21,522
I want to be a cavalryman, like my father was.

8
00:00:22,022 --> 00:00:24,973
Five years later, he'll be dead from a stab wound.

9
00:00:25,193 --> 00:00:29,417
And for almost two hundred years, nobody will agree on who he was.

10
00:00:29,637 --> 00:00:30,664
A lost prince.

11
00:00:30,884 --> 00:00:31,674
A victim.

12
00:00:31,893 --> 00:00:34,832
Or the greatest con artist of the nineteenth century.

13
00:00:36,232 --> 00:00:41,247
Hi, I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and today's file is Kaspar Hauser.

14
00:00:41,467 --> 00:00:43,440
Europe's most famous mystery boy.

15
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:47,308
And this one actually has a twist from twenty twenty four.

16
00:00:47,528 --> 00:00:52,499
A lab that normally studies DNA from Neanderthals took a crack at his hair.

17
00:00:52,719 --> 00:00:53,679
We'll get to that.

18
00:00:54,179 --> 00:00:58,689
But first, let's do what everybody in Nuremberg did in eighteen twenty eight.

19
00:00:58,909 --> 00:01:01,918
Stare at the strange kid, and try to figure him out.

20
00:01:03,318 --> 00:01:08,413
The letter he carried was addressed to the captain of a cavalry squadron in Nuremberg.

21
00:01:08,913 --> 00:01:16,840
It claimed to be from a poor laborer who had been given the boy as a baby, raised him in secret, and never let him leave the house.

22
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:21,172
Now, the letter said, the boy wanted to be a soldier like his father.

23
00:01:21,392 --> 00:01:25,447
And if the captain didn't want him, he could, quote, keep him or hang him.

24
00:01:25,947 --> 00:01:30,263
Which is, let's be honest, a terrible way to write a job reference.

25
00:01:30,763 --> 00:01:39,213
There was a second, shorter note, supposedly from his mother, giving his birthday and saying his father had been a cavalryman who was now dead.

26
00:01:39,433 --> 00:01:42,217
Investigators later noticed something odd.

27
00:01:42,437 --> 00:01:45,014
Both notes seemed to be written by the same hand.

28
00:01:46,414 --> 00:01:52,525
The captain, understandably, had no idea what to do with a confused teenager on his doorstep.

29
00:01:52,745 --> 00:01:54,744
So the boy ended up with the police.

30
00:01:55,244 --> 00:01:56,844
They gave him a pen and paper.

31
00:01:57,064 --> 00:01:59,558
And to everyone's surprise, he wrote a name.

32
00:01:59,778 --> 00:02:00,918
Kaspar Hauser.

33
00:02:01,418 --> 00:02:03,175
He seemed to be about sixteen.

34
00:02:03,395 --> 00:02:07,698
He walked unsteadily, and his feet looked like they'd hardly been used.

35
00:02:07,918 --> 00:02:12,059
He ate only bread and water at first, and other food made him sick.

36
00:02:12,559 --> 00:02:14,931
They put him in a tower jail for vagrants.

37
00:02:15,151 --> 00:02:18,100
Which, as welcome parties go, is not great.

38
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:20,943
And yet the whole town started coming to look at him.

39
00:02:21,163 --> 00:02:24,672
Kaspar Hauser had become Nuremberg's main character.

40
00:02:26,072 --> 00:02:29,791
As he learned more words, Kaspar told an incredible story.

41
00:02:30,291 --> 00:02:33,601
He said he had spent his whole life in a tiny dark room.

42
00:02:33,821 --> 00:02:35,073
He slept on straw.

43
00:02:35,293 --> 00:02:38,950
Every morning he woke up to find bread and water beside him.

44
00:02:39,170 --> 00:02:41,014
He never saw the person who brought it.

45
00:02:41,514 --> 00:02:45,108
His only toys, he said, were a couple of wooden horses.

46
00:02:45,607 --> 00:02:48,186
Shortly before his release, a man appeared.

47
00:02:48,406 --> 00:02:53,155
The man taught him to write his name and say that one sentence about the cavalry.

48
00:02:53,375 --> 00:02:56,905
Then he walked him out of the dark and left him near Nuremberg.

49
00:02:57,405 --> 00:02:58,895
It's a heartbreaking story.

50
00:02:59,115 --> 00:03:02,886
It's also, as we'll see, a story that's very hard to check.

51
00:03:04,286 --> 00:03:12,197
The city placed Kaspar with a teacher and philosopher named Georg Friedrich Daumer, who taught him to read, write and draw.

52
00:03:12,697 --> 00:03:14,068
Kaspar learned fast.

53
00:03:14,288 --> 00:03:18,633
Surprisingly fast for someone who supposedly spent years in a box.

54
00:03:18,853 --> 00:03:20,784
He became a local celebrity.

55
00:03:21,004 --> 00:03:24,172
Visitors, scholars, and the curious came to meet him.

56
00:03:24,672 --> 00:03:26,123
Daumer was fascinated.

57
00:03:26,343 --> 00:03:32,501
He ran experiments on Kaspar's senses and claimed the boy could see in the dark and feel magnets.

58
00:03:32,721 --> 00:03:36,342
Which tells you a little bit about the science standards of the time.

59
00:03:36,842 --> 00:03:40,917
This is the eighteen twenties version of a viral influencer.

60
00:03:41,137 --> 00:03:45,274
Except the followers came over in person and stared at you while you ate.

61
00:03:46,674 --> 00:03:53,863
Then, in October eighteen twenty nine, Kaspar was found in Daumer's cellar, bleeding from a cut on his forehead.

62
00:03:54,363 --> 00:03:56,377
He said a man in a hood had attacked him.

63
00:03:56,597 --> 00:03:59,695
The same man, he claimed, who had kept him in the dark.

64
00:04:00,195 --> 00:04:01,364
The town panicked.

65
00:04:01,584 --> 00:04:04,070
Kaspar was moved and placed under guard.

66
00:04:04,290 --> 00:04:06,092
But some people started to wonder.

67
00:04:06,592 --> 00:04:08,202
The wound was not serious.

68
00:04:08,422 --> 00:04:10,065
Nobody else saw the attacker.

69
00:04:10,285 --> 00:04:15,179
And a few months earlier, Daumer had been growing tired of some of Kaspar's lies.

70
00:04:15,399 --> 00:04:20,463
A lot of observers suspected he had hurt himself, to win back attention and sympathy.

71
00:04:20,963 --> 00:04:24,732
A year later, a pistol went off in his room and grazed his head.

72
00:04:24,952 --> 00:04:26,584
He said it was an accident.

73
00:04:26,804 --> 00:04:28,883
That also didn't help his reputation.

74
00:04:30,283 --> 00:04:33,584
Meanwhile, a much bigger rumor was spreading across Europe.

75
00:04:33,804 --> 00:04:35,789
That Kaspar was secretly royal.

76
00:04:36,289 --> 00:04:37,740
The story went like this.

77
00:04:37,960 --> 00:04:44,014
In eighteen twelve, a baby boy was born to the Grand Duke of Baden, the heir to the throne.

78
00:04:44,234 --> 00:04:45,778
He died a few weeks later.

79
00:04:46,278 --> 00:04:47,116
Or did he?

80
00:04:47,336 --> 00:04:54,795
Rumors claimed the healthy prince had been swapped with a dying baby by someone who wanted a different family branch to inherit.

81
00:04:55,015 --> 00:04:58,531
And then the real prince was hidden away for sixteen years.

82
00:04:59,031 --> 00:05:01,572
It's the plot of about forty soap operas.

83
00:05:01,792 --> 00:05:02,845
And people loved it.

84
00:05:03,065 --> 00:05:05,182
Hauser's supporters wrote books about it.

85
00:05:05,402 --> 00:05:13,657
A famous judge, Anselm von Feuerbach, became convinced Kaspar was the victim of a crime involving powerful people.

86
00:05:15,057 --> 00:05:20,025
Then a wealthy English nobleman, Lord Stanhope, took an interest in Kaspar.

87
00:05:20,245 --> 00:05:23,674
He paid for his care and moved him to the town of Ansbach.

88
00:05:24,174 --> 00:05:27,078
At first Stanhope talked about taking him to England.

89
00:05:27,298 --> 00:05:31,396
But the more time he spent with Kaspar, the less he believed his story.

90
00:05:31,896 --> 00:05:39,298
He even paid for trips to Hungary, to see if Kaspar would recognize words from a language he claimed to half remember.

91
00:05:39,518 --> 00:05:40,581
Nothing came of it.

92
00:05:41,081 --> 00:05:44,542
By the end, Stanhope became one of his loudest skeptics.

93
00:05:44,762 --> 00:05:49,526
Which is the nineteenth century equivalent of your biggest fan unfollowing you.

94
00:05:50,926 --> 00:05:57,150
On December fourteenth, eighteen thirty three, Kaspar came home with a deep stab wound in his chest.

95
00:05:57,650 --> 00:06:04,860
He said a stranger had lured him to the Court Garden in Ansbach, promising news about his mother, and then stabbed him.

96
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:07,495
A small purse was found in the garden.

97
00:06:07,715 --> 00:06:12,383
Inside it was a note written in mirror writing, so it had to be read backwards.

98
00:06:12,603 --> 00:06:15,690
It hinted that the attacker knew where Kaspar came from.

99
00:06:16,190 --> 00:06:18,049
Kaspar died three days later.

100
00:06:18,269 --> 00:06:20,119
He was around twenty one years old.

101
00:06:21,519 --> 00:06:23,587
And even his death became a mystery.

102
00:06:24,087 --> 00:06:26,734
Investigators noticed things that didn't fit.

103
00:06:26,954 --> 00:06:29,146
Only one set of footprints in the snow.

104
00:06:29,366 --> 00:06:32,771
The note had a spelling mistake Kaspar himself often made.

105
00:06:32,991 --> 00:06:36,617
And it was folded in a way he usually folded his letters.

106
00:06:37,117 --> 00:06:44,876
Many concluded he had stabbed himself, maybe intending only a small wound, to repeat the drama of eighteen twenty nine.

107
00:06:45,096 --> 00:06:46,981
Others insisted it was murder.

108
00:06:47,201 --> 00:06:49,751
Doctors who examined the wound couldn't agree.

109
00:06:50,251 --> 00:06:52,581
Either way, a young man died in the snow.

110
00:06:52,801 --> 00:06:54,460
And that part isn't a joke.

111
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:57,922
Whatever he was, his life was strange and lonely.

112
00:06:59,322 --> 00:07:01,043
So can modern science solve it?

113
00:07:01,263 --> 00:07:02,057
It tried.

114
00:07:02,277 --> 00:07:03,243
Three times.

115
00:07:03,743 --> 00:07:12,324
In nineteen ninety six, scientists tested blood from Kaspar's underwear, kept in a museum, and compared it with the Baden family.

116
00:07:12,544 --> 00:07:13,367
No match.

117
00:07:13,867 --> 00:07:18,667
In the early two thousands, a different team tested hair and found a close match.

118
00:07:18,887 --> 00:07:21,465
Supporters of the prince theory celebrated.

119
00:07:21,965 --> 00:07:31,953
Then in twenty twenty four, a team led by forensic scientist Walther Parson in Innsbruck used methods built for ancient DNA on Kaspar's hair.

120
00:07:32,173 --> 00:07:33,955
A second lab checked it blind.

121
00:07:34,175 --> 00:07:38,811
The result, Kaspar's maternal DNA does not match the Baden family.

122
00:07:39,311 --> 00:07:45,240
So, after almost two hundred years, the prince theory is, scientifically speaking, dethroned.

123
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:47,643
So what's left?

124
00:07:48,143 --> 00:07:49,024
Theory one.

125
00:07:49,244 --> 00:07:53,102
A real victim of abuse and confinement, just not a prince.

126
00:07:53,322 --> 00:07:56,598
Someone who was kept hidden for reasons we'll never know.

127
00:07:57,098 --> 00:07:58,020
Theory two.

128
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:05,257
A confused or troubled young man who exaggerated or invented his past, and enjoyed the attention it brought.

129
00:08:05,477 --> 00:08:16,564
A twenty twenty three study even found vaccination scars that suggest he was vaccinated against smallpox as a child, which doesn't fit a lifetime alone in the dark.

130
00:08:17,064 --> 00:08:18,018
Theory three.

131
00:08:18,238 --> 00:08:19,598
Some mixture of both.

132
00:08:19,818 --> 00:08:25,609
A neglected kid with a sad past who discovered that a dramatic story made people care.

133
00:08:26,109 --> 00:08:28,471
Honestly, theory three is the most human.

134
00:08:28,691 --> 00:08:30,579
And the hardest to put on a headline.

135
00:08:31,979 --> 00:08:33,704
So here's the honest version.

136
00:08:34,204 --> 00:08:35,142
Documented.

137
00:08:35,362 --> 00:08:41,258
In May eighteen twenty eight, a teenage boy appeared in Nuremberg with two notes and few words.

138
00:08:41,478 --> 00:08:52,816
He became famous, was cared for by Daumer and later Lord Stanhope, was wounded in eighteen twenty nine, and died of a stab wound in Ansbach in December eighteen thirty three.

139
00:08:53,036 --> 00:08:56,842
Modern DNA testing rules out the Baden prince theory.

140
00:08:57,342 --> 00:08:58,200
Disputed.

141
00:08:58,420 --> 00:09:00,174
His childhood in the dark cell.

142
00:09:00,394 --> 00:09:03,193
Whether the attacks were real or self inflicted.

143
00:09:03,413 --> 00:09:06,638
Whether his death was murder, suicide or an accident.

144
00:09:07,138 --> 00:09:07,867
Legend.

145
00:09:08,087 --> 00:09:11,472
That he was a stolen prince, and that he could see in the dark.

146
00:09:12,872 --> 00:09:13,984
So, your verdict.

147
00:09:14,204 --> 00:09:16,662
Victim, con artist, or a bit of both?

148
00:09:17,162 --> 00:09:18,407
Tell me in the comments.

149
00:09:18,627 --> 00:09:21,214
His gravestone calls him the riddle of his time.

150
00:09:21,434 --> 00:09:26,394
Two hundred years later, that's still the most accurate thing anyone has written about him.

151
00:09:26,894 --> 00:09:30,972
If you like true stories that history never quite finished, subscribe.

152
00:09:31,192 --> 00:09:35,574
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and I'll see you in the next file.
